{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before a library closes, when the light turns amber and every sound becomes softer than it should. People gather their notebooks slowly, as if reluctant to admit that the day has finally spent itself. Outside, the street is wet from a rain nobody noticed falling, and the buses sigh against the curb with tired, patient brakes. I like to stay until the last announcement, pretending to search for one more sentence, one more footnote that might explain everything. It never arrives, of course, but the pretending matters because it gives curiosity a place to sit down. On the walk home I pass bakeries closing their shutters, cats negotiating territory on warm hoods, and windows lighting up one by one. Each window suggests a life briefly glimpsed and immediately imagined, complete with arguments, recipes, unfinished letters, and plants leaning toward impossible sun. I do not envy those lives; I simply borrow their glow for a block or two, then return it without asking permission. At home, I make tea too strong to drink comfortably and read whatever book followed me back, its spine cracked like a familiar road. Some nights the pages make perfect sense; other nights they seem written for a stranger I might become next year, or never. That uncertainty feels honest, kinder than certainty, and I fall asleep grateful for questions that refuse to hurry toward their answers. Morning will rearrange them gently, and I will begin again, willingly, without proof.",
